Template: portikus/welcome
Type: note
Description: Welcome to Portikus
 Portikus gives each student a development workspace in the browser, with
 terminals, an editor and AI coding assistants, running in containers on
 this server.
 .
 The next screens ask for this server's web address, the administrator's
 email, how people sign in, and where to keep student files. Each one
 suggests an answer.
 .
 The site starts with a certificate from Portikus's own authority. Choose
 a public one later on the Certificate page of the administration screen.
 .
 Nothing on this server changes until you confirm on the last screen.
 Setup then runs in the background and takes about ten minutes.

Template: portikus/error
Type: note
Description: Please check that answer
 ${message}

Template: portikus/public_host
Type: string
Description: Web address of this server:
 The host name people type in their browser to reach Portikus, such as
 portikus.example.edu. It should already point at this server in DNS.
 .
 Student previews use names under preview.<this name>.

Template: portikus/admin_email
Type: string
Description: Email of the Portikus administrator:
 Setup creates a local administrator account with this email and a
 one-time password, which you change at first sign-in. The address does
 not need to receive mail.

Template: portikus/tls
Type: select
Choices-C: internal, letsencrypt, files
Choices: Portikus's own certificate authority, Let's Encrypt through Cloudflare DNS, Certificate files I already have
Default: internal
Description: HTTPS certificate:
 Portikus is served only over HTTPS. Choose where its first certificate
 comes from. After setup, change it on the Certificate page of the
 administration screen; setup never changes it again.
 .
 Own authority: works without public DNS, but every browser must be told
 to trust its root certificate, which the Certificate page offers.
 .
 Let's Encrypt: needs your domain's DNS at Cloudflare and a Cloudflare API
 token. A free certificate every browser trusts.
 .
 Certificate files: a certificate and key you already have. They must
 cover this name and *.preview.<this name>.

Template: portikus/acme_email
Type: string
Description: Email for Let's Encrypt:
 Let's Encrypt writes to this address if a certificate is about to expire
 or has a problem.

Template: portikus/cloudflare_api_token
Type: password
Description: Cloudflare API token:
 Paste a Cloudflare API token with the "Edit zone DNS" permission for this
 domain.
 .
 Student previews need a wildcard certificate, which Let's Encrypt issues
 only after a DNS check. Portikus makes that check through Cloudflare, so
 your domain's DNS must be hosted there.
 .
 The token is stored in /etc/portikus/secrets.yaml, which only root can
 read, and under /etc/portikus/certificate, which only root and Caddy can
 read. ${keep}

Template: portikus/tls_cert
Type: string
Description: Certificate file:
 The full path to the certificate on this server, in PEM format, with any
 intermediate certificates after it.

Template: portikus/tls_key
Type: string
Description: Private key file:
 The full path to the certificate's private key on this server, in PEM
 format. Keep it readable by root only.

Template: portikus/provider
Type: select
Choices-C: dex, entra, google, ldap, oidc
Choices: Local accounts only, Microsoft Entra ID, Google Workspace, LDAP or Active Directory, Another OpenID Connect provider
Default: dex
Description: How people sign in:
 Portikus always has local accounts, which the administrator creates. It
 can also let people sign in with your institution's accounts.
 .
 Local accounts only: nothing more to set up now. You can add single
 sign-on later with "dpkg-reconfigure portikus".
 .
 Microsoft Entra ID or Google Workspace: people use their school accounts.
 Register Portikus as an application there first.
 .
 LDAP or Active Directory: people use their directory accounts.
 .
 Another OpenID Connect provider: such as Okta, Keycloak or Shibboleth.

Template: portikus/entra_tenant_id
Type: string
Description: Entra tenant ID:
 The Directory (tenant) ID of your organization, shown on the app
 registration's Overview page. It looks like
 12345678-90ab-cdef-1234-567890abcdef.

Template: portikus/google_domains
Type: string
Description: Google Workspace domains:
 The domains whose accounts may sign in, such as example.edu. Separate
 more than one with spaces.

Template: portikus/oidc_issuer
Type: string
Description: Provider's issuer URL:
 The provider's HTTPS issuer address, such as
 https://login.example.edu/realms/main. Portikus finds the rest from it.

Template: portikus/client_id
Type: string
Description: Client ID:
 The client (application) ID your provider gave Portikus when you
 registered it. The redirect address to register there is
 https://${host}/dex/callback.

Template: portikus/client_secret
Type: password
Description: Client secret:
 Paste the client secret of that registration.
 .
 It is stored only in /etc/portikus/secrets.yaml, which only root can
 read. ${keep}

Template: portikus/oidc_groups_claim
Type: string
Default: groups
Description: Groups claim:
 The name of the claim in the provider's tokens that lists a person's
 groups. Most providers use "groups".

Template: portikus/ldap_host
Type: string
Description: Directory server:
 The directory's host name, with a port if it is not 636, such as
 ad.example.edu or ldap.example.edu:3269. Portikus always connects over
 TLS.

Template: portikus/ldap_schema
Type: select
Choices-C: ad, openldap
Choices: Active Directory, OpenLDAP or another LDAP server
Default: ad
Description: Kind of directory:
 This picks the attribute names Portikus reads for user names, emails and
 groups.

Template: portikus/ldap_ip_allow
Type: string
Description: Directory server addresses:
 The IP addresses or networks of the directory servers, such as 10.0.0.5
 or 10.0.0.0/24. Separate more than one with spaces. The sign-in service
 may connect only to these.

Template: portikus/ldap_bind_dn
Type: string
Description: Service account:
 The distinguished name Portikus signs in to the directory as, to look
 people up, such as cn=portikus,ou=Services,dc=example,dc=edu.

Template: portikus/ldap_bind_password
Type: password
Description: Service account password:
 Type the password of the service account.
 .
 It is stored only in /etc/portikus/secrets.yaml, which only root can
 read. ${keep}

Template: portikus/ldap_user_base_dn
Type: string
Description: Where people are:
 The part of the directory to search for people, such as
 ou=People,dc=example,dc=edu.

Template: portikus/ldap_user_filter
Type: string
Description: Who may sign in:
 An LDAP filter, in parentheses, naming who may sign in at all. Without a
 narrow filter every account in the directory could, so name a group, such
 as (memberOf=cn=portikus-users,ou=Groups,dc=example,dc=edu). The group
 questions later only decide each person's role.

Template: portikus/ldap_group_base_dn
Type: string
Description: Where groups are (optional):
 The part of the directory to search for groups, such as
 ou=Groups,dc=example,dc=edu. Leave it blank to skip group lookups; then
 everyone signs in as a student and the administrator assigns other roles.

Template: portikus/ldap_root_ca
Type: string
Description: Directory's CA certificate (optional):
 The full path to the certificate authority file that signed the
 directory's certificate, if this server does not already trust it. Leave
 it blank to use the system's trusted authorities.

Template: portikus/student_group
Type: string
Default: portikus-students
Description: Students' group:
 The group whose members sign in as students. ${hint}
 .
 ${rule}

Template: portikus/instructor_group
Type: string
Default: portikus-instructors
Description: Instructors' group:
 The group whose members sign in as instructors. ${hint}

Template: portikus/admin_group
Type: string
Default: portikus-administrators
Description: Administrators' group:
 The group whose members sign in as administrators. ${hint}

Template: portikus/storage
Type: select
Choices-C: ${values}
Choices: ${labels}
Description: Where to keep student files:
 Student files, containers and Docker data live in one storage pool.
 .
 An empty disk is fastest; setup erases it. An LVM volume group gives its
 free space and leaves existing volumes alone. A file on the main disk
 needs no spare disk, but is slower and shares space with the system.

Template: portikus/storage_confirm
Type: boolean
Default: false
Description: Erase ${device}?
 EVERYTHING ON ${device} (${size}) WILL BE ERASED when setup runs. This
 cannot be undone.
 .
 Choose Yes only if that disk holds nothing you need. Choose No to decide
 later: setup will not touch the disk until you say Yes.

Template: portikus/storage_size
Type: string
Description: Size of the storage file, in GiB:
 Student files go in one file on this server's main disk. It is slower
 than a disk of its own and shares space with the system.
 .
 The main disk has ${free} GiB free. Give a whole number of GiB, at most
 ${max}, so the system keeps room to work.

Template: portikus/registry_cache_gib
Type: string
Default: 20
Description: Size of the Docker image cache, in GiB:
 Students' Docker Hub and ghcr.io pulls go through a cache on this server, so an image
 one student pulled is not downloaded again for the next. It is a file of
 this size on the main disk, which the main disk must have room for.
 .
 The main disk has ${free} GiB free. Give a whole number of GiB, such as
 20. A different size later empties the cache. If the disk has too little
 room when setup runs, setup makes the cache smaller, or turns it off.

Template: portikus/summary
Type: boolean
Default: true
Description: Save these answers and start setup?
 ${summary}
 .
 Yes saves them in /etc/portikus/portikus.yaml, with secrets in
 /etc/portikus/secrets.yaml, and starts setup in the background. No goes
 back to change an answer.
